274
00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:04.349
All right.

275
00:17:04.349 --> 00:17:10.349
So let's go back to the slides here and talk about some pro tips involved when it comes to your 3D LUTs.

276
00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:13.349
And then finally, let's take a look at this real quick.

277
00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:14.349
So pro tips.

278
00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:19.349
Ensure that the same viewport or node is not included in multiple OSAO overrides.

279
00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:21.349
And you heard me talk about that.

280
00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:25.349
So you can easily stack this if you're not paying attention on accident.

281
00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:31.349
You can end up loading a color profile like we did in a post process volume over a 3D LUT.

282
00:17:31.349 --> 00:17:35.349
You can end up loading this in your end display.

283
00:17:35.349 --> 00:17:39.450
You can end up having it in the camera itself.

284
00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:40.450
So you've got to be careful.

285
00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:43.450
You have to make sure that you know where everything's at.

286
00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:48.450
So you're not accidentally overriding or tweaking your color information unnecessarily.

287
00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:52.609
Additionally, color grading can be applied with the end display, which we mentioned,

288
00:17:52.609 --> 00:17:57.609
and is the preferred way to go, especially in 5.0.

289
00:17:57.609 --> 00:18:02.609
And that may evolve to a higher level later on with new versions of Unreal.

290
00:18:02.609 --> 00:18:05.609
So in shuffling your color work, like we mentioned,

291
00:18:05.609 --> 00:18:09.609
keep in mind how the color changes based on the order of operation.

292
00:18:09.609 --> 00:18:16.769
So set your post process volume for optimal output to tone curve amount to zero.

293
00:18:16.769 --> 00:18:18.769
Your expand gamut to zero.

294
00:18:18.769 --> 00:18:20.769
Your blue correction to zero.

295
00:18:20.769 --> 00:18:22.769
Your chromatic aberration to zero.

296
00:18:22.769 --> 00:18:24.769
And vignetting to zero.

297
00:18:24.769 --> 00:18:25.769
And bluing to zero.

298
00:18:25.769 --> 00:18:27.769
Those are just good practices.

299
00:18:27.769 --> 00:18:28.769
And you can leave those on.

300
00:18:28.769 --> 00:18:32.769
And that allows you, if you needed to go edit them later for any particular reason,

301
00:18:32.769 --> 00:18:34.769
they're available and they're ready to go.

302
00:18:34.769 --> 00:18:39.769
So again, how this is approached processing wise.

303
00:18:39.769 --> 00:18:43.769
And I keep mentioning this because it's really good to understand how this works.

304
00:18:43.769 --> 00:18:47.769
So color processing happens in this order based on your approach.

305
00:18:47.769 --> 00:18:50.769
Lighting to produce scene color.

306
00:18:50.769 --> 00:18:54.180
Post process material before tone mapper.

307
00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:59.279
Tone mapper, which includes the tone curve and bloom and vignette,

308
00:18:59.279 --> 00:19:01.279
or replace tone mapper.

309
00:19:01.279 --> 00:19:07.339
OCIO, which turns off the tone curve if replace tone mapper is not used.

310
00:19:07.339 --> 00:19:11.339
And again, post process material after tone mapper.

311
00:19:12.339 --> 00:19:17.339
So this is basically the order of how things can interrupt with each other.

312
00:19:17.339 --> 00:19:19.599
So think of it that way.

313
00:19:19.599 --> 00:19:23.599
So if you don't consider this order, you actually can't overwrite your stuff.

314
00:19:23.599 --> 00:19:25.599
This is how things are processed.

315
00:19:25.599 --> 00:19:29.599
So think of it that way on my list here.

316
00:19:29.599 --> 00:19:31.599
I hope all of you enjoyed this course.

317
00:19:31.599 --> 00:19:34.599
And feel free to let us know if you have any other questions.
